---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { CONTACT_NOTE, SITE } from "../lib/site.mjs";

const description =
  "Draft privacy notice for XHere, a project by Steven Sills II. What the app and this website handle, and how to delete an account.";
---

<Base title="Privacy" description={description} draft>
  <h1>Privacy</h1>
  <p>
    XHere is a project by {SITE.owner}. It is not a company, and this draft does not invent an address, a phone number, or a registration.
    Questions go to <a href={`mailto:${SITE.contactEmail}`}>{SITE.contactEmail}</a>. {CONTACT_NOTE}
  </p>

  <h2>What XHere is</h2>
  <p>
    XHere lets someone attach a short drop to a real-world place and lets other people find it.
    The pilot city is Jamestown, New York. The app is meant to work anywhere.
    This website is the public face: a landing page, share links, and these policy pages. It is not the map app.
  </p>

  <h2>This website</h2>
  <p>
    The pages you are reading are static. They do not set cookies, and they do not run analytics.
    The tester form opens an email to the placeholder address. It does not store what you type on a server.
    A share link shows a teaser: title, category, and a map image. It does not show the hidden text of a drop.
    Sensitive drops (safety alerts and incident reports) also hide the author’s name and show only an approximate area.
  </p>
  <p>
    Map images are drawn from OpenFreeMap vector tiles, the same free tiles the Android app loads with MapLibre.
    Those tiles come from OpenStreetMap and OpenMapTiles. Fetching a tile sends the usual connection data (such as an IP address) to that tile service.
    XHere does not add an account or an API key to that request.
  </p>

  <h2>The Android app</h2>
  <p>When you use the app, it handles:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Account.</strong> An email address if you sign in with a one-time code, or the basic profile Google shares if you use Google sign-in, plus a handle you pick.</li>
    <li><strong>What you post.</strong> The title, text, category, tags, and photos of drops you create, and confirmations or reports you submit.</li>
    <li><strong>Location, while the app is open, if you allow it.</strong> The app asks only for foreground location. It uses that position to show drops near you, to place a drop you create, and to measure whether you are close enough to reveal or confirm one. It does not ask for background location.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The app is not supposed to keep a history of where you walked. A confirmation stores the distance, not a trail.
    For categories that require a blurred location, other people receive the blurred point, not the exact one you stood on.
    Incident reports are blurred to about 100 meters.
  </p>
  <p>
    There is no hosted database yet. Until {SITE.owner} approves a free Supabase project, the app can run against a local preview and this website shows built-in Jamestown examples.
    When that project exists, account and drop data will sit in its Postgres database. The service-role key stays off the website and out of the app.
  </p>

  <h2>What other people can see</h2>
  <p>
    Other people can see your handle, unless a drop is shown anonymously or the category is sensitive on the web.
    They can see the teaser of your drop from far away, and the rest only when they are close enough.
    They cannot see your exact location history, your email, or the hidden text of a locked drop.
  </p>

  <h2>How long it stays</h2>
  <p>
    Some drops expire on their own (a food truck lasts hours, an incident report lasts 72 hours, a note lasts 7 days unless people confirm it).
    If you delete your account, the app hides your profile immediately and a scheduled job permanently deletes the login within 30 days.
    See <a href="/delete-account">Delete account</a> for the steps and for what is removed.
  </p>

  <h2>Children</h2>
  <p>XHere is not directed at children under 13, and it does not ask for their information.</p>

  <h2>Selling data</h2>
  <p>This project does not sell personal information. This draft does not add advertising or a data broker.</p>

  <h2>Changes</h2>
  <p>
    {SITE.owner} will replace this draft before the site is the public privacy policy.
    The date of that approved version will be written here. Until then, this page is a draft for his review.
  </p>
</Base>
